course: dictionary
lesson: 9952
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word taste has more than one meaning, so you must read the sentence to find the right one. «Lick a lemon and it is sour; taste a tiny pinch of baking soda and it is bitter and soapy.» Here taste is a verb: it means to try a little food or drink to find its flavour.
explain: Look at the words around it. «She has good taste in music» has nothing to do with food. Taste here means what a person likes. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "liking" in place of taste, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Taste can also be a noun for a flavour, as in «a sweet taste», or a small try, as in «a taste of life in Canada». The clues around the word always tell you which meaning the author means.
fact: Taste is one of the five senses. Your tongue can sense sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury flavours.
fact: Smell helps taste. «When a cold blocks your nose, your dinner tastes of almost nothing», because much of what we call flavour is really smell.
fact: Taste can mean what a person likes, as in «good taste» or «bad taste».
fact: Never taste unknown liquids, berries, mushrooms or medicines. Ask a grown-up first.
fact: «Monarchs taste bad to birds» because their caterpillars eat poisonous milkweed.
exercise: «Lick a lemon and it is sour; taste a tiny pinch of baking soda and it is bitter and soapy.» What does taste mean in this sentence? | Wash it in cold water | Throw it away | Cook it for a long time | *Try a tiny amount to find its flavour
exercise: In «a sweet taste», what does taste mean? | A loud noise | A slow walk | *The flavour of food or drink in your mouth | Good manners
exercise: «She has good taste in music.» What does taste mean here? | *What a person likes | To sing softly | The flavour of music | A tiny try of music
test: Which sense lets you notice flavours, mainly with your tongue? | Hearing | Sight | *Taste | Touch
test: Why can a bad cold make your dinner taste of almost nothing? | *Smell helps taste, and a blocked nose means less flavour | Your tongue grows bigger | The food is poisonous | The food has gone cold
exam: Which should you never do with unknown berries or mushrooms? | Count them | *Taste them | Draw them | Look at them
